\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\title{Fermionic elimination}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{What remains after some fermion variables are eliminated?
The [paired Grassmann integral](fdip-008H) replaces a Gaussian
normalizer by a determinant, but the effective quadratic form is
still a [Schur complement](fdip-000D). The eliminated block must
be invertible; the full matrix need not be positive or invertible
for this algebraic identity.}

\p{A commuting complex field supplies the inverse determinant
when the Hermitian part of its matrix is positive. This is an
ordinary convergent integral on twice as many real coordinates.
Its cancellation with the Grassmann determinant uses the same
matrix in both quadratic forms. A non-Hermitian accretive matrix
usually gives a complex weight, so this cancellation does not
construct a positive joint probability law.}

\p{The logarithm of the determinant needs an additional choice.
On the accretive cone there is a unique analytic logarithm
normalized at the identity; it need not be the principal scalar
logarithm of the determinant. The [four-cycle](fdip-000F) makes
these distinctions concrete with the same effective matrix as
in real Gaussian elimination.}

\transclude{fdip-009J}
